WEBVTT

00:00:00.650 --> 00:00:02.770
A force is a push or pull.

00:00:02.950 --> 00:00:06.070
Stress is force divided by the area over which it acts,

00:00:06.190 --> 00:00:09.650
so the same force on a smaller area produces greater stress.

00:00:09.970 --> 00:00:12.850
Confining pressure acts equally from every direction;

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:15.550
differential stress differs with direction.

00:00:16.230 --> 00:00:20.130
Rock may translate to a new position, rotate to a new

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:22.790
orientation, or change its shape and size.

00:00:22.970 --> 00:00:24.690
That internal change is strain.

00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:26.870
A tilted bed records rotation;

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:29.470
flattened pebbles record internal strain.

00:00:29.770 --> 00:00:31.750
More than one response can occur together.

00:00:32.330 --> 00:00:36.790
On the supplied strength graph, depth increases downward and strength increases

00:00:36.790 --> 00:00:41.210
to the right. In shallow brittle rock, confinement generally increases resistance

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:46.090
to breaking. Deeper, hotter rock can deform by solid-state flow and

00:00:46.090 --> 00:00:49.450
strength decreases. The curve peaks near the transition.

00:00:50.330 --> 00:00:54.590
The brittle–ductile transition is a change in dominant behavior, not a

00:00:54.590 --> 00:00:56.810
universal fixed depth or a melting line.

00:00:57.210 --> 00:01:01.650
Temperature, composition, fluids, pressure, and strain rate affect how a rock

00:01:01.650 --> 00:01:05.550
responds. The graph is a model, not a single depth that

00:01:05.550 --> 00:01:06.390
applies everywhere.
